  the SN-only band is 10-40x wider than the band the record quotes.  The record's band is a BAO+CMB
  band that supernovae only help to close; it is not a supernova measurement of a0(z), and this lane
  finds no way to make it one.

  [FAIL] C3a supernovae ALONE can distinguish the framework's a0(z) from a constant, on the CANONICAL (rho_Lambda) footing   (Delta chi2 = 0.52 for 2 parameters (77% probability by chance); the SN-only 1-sigma profile band at z = 2.5 is [0.36, 3.59], width 3.23, and contains 1.000)
  [PASS] C3b the SAME question on the ALT (rho_total) footing -- and here the answer FLIPS: because that branch says a0 is proportional to H(z), the supernovae pin it directly and to a few per cent   (SN-only profile 1-sigma a0(z)/a0(0) = [1.69, 2.15] at z = 1 and [3.69, 4.72] at z = 2.5, both excluding a constant by a wide margin)
  Both footings, plainly, because the difference is the whole point: on the canonical footing the
  supernovae say nothing about a0(z), because the ratio depends only on the dark-energy sector they
  barely constrain.  On the alt footing they say a great deal -- but only because a0 proportional to
  H(z) is the Hubble diagram itself, so the 'prediction' carries no content the expansion history did
  not already have.  The alt footing converts supernovae from a spectator into a precision predictor,
  and section D asks whether the galaxies agree.

  C4 -- where the record's constraint actually comes from.  Add ONLY a CMB-like matter-density prior
        (Omega_m = 0.315 +/- 0.007) to the same supernovae:
  SN + Om prior best fit: w0 = -0.95, wa = -0.12; Delta chi2 vs w = -1 is 0.66 for 2 parameters
    z = 1.0: profile 1-sigma a0(z)/a0(0) in [0.907, 1.115]  (SN-only was [0.707, 1.760])
    z = 2.5: profile 1-sigma a0(z)/a0(0) in [0.654, 1.354]  (SN-only was [0.362, 3.588])
    z = 3.0: profile 1-sigma a0(z)/a0(0) in [0.593, 1.430]  (SN-only was [0.299, 4.368])
  even with the matter density pinned to CMB precision the supernovae do not exclude a constant a0.
  The framework's a0(z) departure on the record is inherited from DESI's BAO geometry combined with
  the CMB, NOT from supernovae; the SNe contribute by breaking the (Om, w0, wa) degeneracy, not by
  detecting evolution themselves.  Stated against interest: this makes the whole distinctive
  prediction hostage to a BAO+CMB preference that supernovae, on their own, do not corroborate.

======================================================================================================================
D -- TARGET 3: the rho_Lambda / rho_total footing fork inside the supernova redshift range
======================================================================================================================
  The headline equation a0 = kappa c sqrt(G rho) is ambiguous in rho.  The two readings are NOT two
  normalisations of one curve -- they are two DIFFERENT functions of redshift:
  z = 0:  a0 = 9.3619e-11 (rho_Lambda) vs 1.1279e-10 m/s^2 (rho_total) -- a fixed 0.0809 dex offset
  z > 0:  the canonical branch is bump-then-decline; the alt branch is a0 proportional to H(z), a
          monotone rise.  The separation grows without limit.

      z |  canonical a0(z)/a0(0) |   alt a0(z)/a0(0) |  separation [dex]
   0.33 |                 1.0354 |            1.2147 |             0.069
   0.50 |                 1.0319 |            1.3389 |             0.113
   0.90 |                 0.9996 |            1.6867 |             0.227
   1.00 |                 0.9889 |            1.7860 |             0.257
   1.44 |                 0.9381 |            2.2757 |             0.385
   1.68 |                 0.9100 |            2.5750 |             0.452
   2.00 |                 0.8739 |            3.0047 |             0.536
   2.50 |                 0.8217 |            3.7374 |             0.658
   3.00 |                 0.7750 |            4.5356 |             0.767
  (evaluated on DESI DR2 + CMB + Pantheon+; on w = -1 the canonical column is exactly 1.000 and the
   separation is log10 E(z), 0.253 dex at z = 1 -- the fork is not a dark-energy artefact.)

  D2 -- can the supernova-era a0 measurements already in the repo separate the two branches?

  MUSE-DARK III (Ciocan et al. 2026, A&A 709 L16): 79 galaxies, 0.33 < z < 1.44, a0(z) = a0(0)+a1 z,
    a0(0) = 1.00 +/- 0.04, a1 = +1.59 (+0.11/-0.10) x 1e-10, a0(z~1) = 2.38 (+0.12/-0.10) x 1e-10,
    intrinsic scatter 0.17 dex.  The authors state reconciliation to a constant a0 would need stellar
    masses larger by +0.20 to +0.45 dex, which they say is NOT supported by their consistency checks.
    at z = 1 the two branches predict 0.989 and 1.786, a separation of 0.257 dex.
    MUSE's own formal error on a0(z~1)/a0(0) is 0.027 dex (including its a0(0) error) -- 10x smaller than
    the separation, so on FORMAL errors the branches are separable and BOTH are rejected (the data sit
    at 0.377 dex, above both).  But in deep MOND a0 = V^4/(G M_b), so d log a0 =
    -d log M_b exactly: the authors' own disowned-but-required M/L offset of 0.20-0.45 dex
    propagates 1:1 and is 0.8-1.8x the branch separation.
    Adding the quoted 0.17 dex intrinsic scatter in quadrature, the systematic floor at z = 1 is
    0.26-0.48 dex against a 0.26 dex separation.

  MSA-3D (NIRSpec, this repo's inversion; 23 galaxies, 0.58 < z < 1.68): the raw a0-trend +2.1 splits
    into +1.13 pure acceleration-selection and +1.00 from f_DM; controlling for g_obs the genuine
    trend is +0.91 [+0.05, +1.63] per unit z in units of 1e-10, ~1.1 sigma from flat.
    over its own 0.58 < z < 1.68 baseline the branches predict fractional slopes -0.10 (canonical)
    and +0.76 (alt) per unit z: a 0.86 separation against MSA-3D's controlled
    uncertainty of +/-0.79 -> 1.1 sigma.

  [FAIL] D2 the supernova-era a0 measurements already in the repo (MUSE-DARK III, MSA-3D) can separate the rho_Lambda footing from the rho_total footing   (MUSE systematic floor 0.26-0.48 dex vs a 0.26 dex separation; MSA-3D 1.1 sigma on the slope. Formal errors say yes, the stated systematics say no.)
  Direction stated: this is a FAIL on the systematics, not on the physics.  The branch separation at
  z = 1 is large (a factor 1.8); it is the a0 = V^4/(G M_b) readout that hands the stellar-mass
  systematic straight into the answer at 1:1.  A gas-dominated, rotation-dominated deep-MOND target
  with an independent gas mass -- i.e. exactly the z ~ 2.5 object already pre-registered -- separates
  the branches by 0.66 dex, five times the registered 0.13 dex budget.  The footing fork is the
  EASIEST thing that measurement decides, and it is not what the pre-registration scores.

  D3 -- WHICH WAY DOES THE SUPERNOVA-ERA ARCHIVE LEAN?  'Cannot separate' is not 'says nothing', so
        the direction is reported -- but it MUST be reported over the whole archive, not over the two
        measurements the lane brief named.  A first pass here used only the two SLOPE measurements and
        produced a clean lean toward the rejected footing.  That was one-sided and is withdrawn: the
        repo's own committed placement script already puts SIX points on this fork, and the RATIO
        points go the other way.  Corrected version below.
  point                                       currency  sigma from canonical  sigma from alt            nearer
  MSA-3D selection-corrected  z 0.58-1.68        slope                  1.45            0.32               alt
  MUSE-DARK III / Ciocan     z 0.33-1.44         slope                  4.10            1.50               alt
  MUSE-DARK II / Jeanneau    z ~ 0.9             ratio                  0.00            1.10    canonical/flat
  Ubler+17 KMOS3D            z ~ 0.9             ratio                  0.79            0.48               alt
  Ubler+17 KMOS3D            z ~ 2.3             ratio                  0.68            1.05    canonical/flat
  Amvrosiadis+25 DSFG        z ~ 2.4             ratio                  0.79            1.40    canonical/flat
  (all six placements verbatim from the repo's own prep_2026/a0z_crossscale/highz_a0z_fork_placement_2026.py,
   re-run 2026-09-08, exit 0.  Its own verdict on every one of them: UNDERPOWERED, excludes no branch.)
  tally: 3 nearer the alt (rho_total) branch, 3 nearer canonical/flat; every point is below 1.5 sigma except Ciocan's slope on inflated systematics (4.1 sigma from canonical).
  [FAIL] D3 the supernova-era a0 archive leans consistently toward ONE footing, so the fork can at least be given a direction   (it does not: 3 of 6 points sit nearer the alt branch and 3 nearer canonical, and the split is BY MEASUREMENT CURRENCY -- both SLOPE points lean alt, and the ratio points are 3-1 the other way)
  What that pattern means, stated carefully and not over-read: a genuine a0(z) would move slope and
  ratio estimators together.  A redshift-dependent SYSTEMATIC in the slope estimators -- pressure
  support, beam smearing, or an M/L drift, all of which grow with z and all of which the systematics-
  floor script already prices -- would move the slopes and leave the ratios alone.  The observed split
  is the second pattern's signature, not the first's.  But every point is under 1.5 sigma, so this is
  an OBSERVATION about the archive's shape and not a measurement of anything.  The two slope points the
  lane brief named do lean toward the branch the record rejects (Ciocan 1.5 vs 4.1 sigma, MSA-3D 0.32
  vs 1.45 sigma); the repo's MUSE confrontation document already records the SIGN match and calls the
  rejected branch an undershoot.  Nothing here is new evidence for the rho_total footing, which the
  coefficient-footing audit rejects on independent z = 0 grounds.

  D4 -- the CONSTRUCTIVE consequence: where the fork becomes decidable with today's systematics.
      z   branch separation [dex]   TODAY coherent floor [dex]   FUTURE floor [dex]   decidable?
    0.5                     0.113                        0.215                0.141           no
    1.0                     0.257                        0.253                0.160           no
    2.0                     0.536                        0.315                0.197  yes, future
    3.0                     0.767                        0.365                0.231    YES today
  (coherent, non-averaging floor for a gas-dominated deep-MOND dwarf, read live from the repo's own
   prep_2026/a0z_crossscale/highz_systematics_floor_results.json; 'decidable' = separation > 2x floor)
  [PASS] D4 the rho_Lambda / rho_total footing fork is decidable somewhere inside the supernova redshift range with TODAY's coherent systematic floor   (yes, at z >= 3.0: the branches are 0.77 dex apart against a 0.36 dex coherent floor)
  This is the lane's one constructive output: the supernovae give the alt branch a sharp, few-per-cent
  prediction (C3b), the branches separate by 0.54-0.77 dex at z = 2-3, and that exceeds twice the
  repo's own coherent floor at z = 3 (and twice the FUTURE floor already at z = 2).  The SAME z ~ 2.5
  object already pre-registered for the 0.00-vs-+0.33 test settles the internal footing fork as a free
  by-product: 0.66 dex of separation against the registered 0.13 dex budget (5x), or against the
  generic uncurated-dwarf coherent floor of ~0.34 dex (2x).  Worth adding to the pre-registration's
  stated deliverables by amendment -- it costs no extra observing time.

======================================================================================================================
E -- TARGET 4: the gravitational-lensing signal in supernova Hubble residuals
======================================================================================================================
  In this framework there are no dark haloes: the lensing convergence toward a supernova is sourced by
  baryons plus the kernel.  Pantheon+ models the lensing scatter as sigma_lens = A x 0.055 z mag with
  A = 1 (Jonsson et al. 2010).  If A is measurable, the mass distribution is probed on Mpc scales.
  error budget decomposition (medians): the non-fit, non-peculiar-velocity variance is 0.0290 mag^2
    = an intrinsic/bias-correction floor of ~0.170 mag; the lensing term (0.055 z)^2 is 0.00179 mag^2 at z > 0.7
    z in [0.1,0.4): N =  652, lensing is  0.46% of the per-SN variance
    z in [0.4,0.7): N =  233, lensing is  1.58% of the per-SN variance
    z in [0.7,1.2): N =   56, lensing is  2.66% of the per-SN variance
    z in [1.2,2.3): N =   19, lensing is  5.59% of the per-SN variance
  profile likelihood on the lensing amplitude A (intrinsic floor free, 960 SNe at z > 0.1,
    diagonal errors -- the covariance is a model for the MEAN, the scatter test is per-SN):
    A_hat = 0.00 (at the physical boundary A >= 0);  1-sigma upper 0.52;  95% upper 1.02.
    Nominal A = 1 is disfavoured by Delta chi2 = 3.94; A = 0 by Delta chi2 = 0.00 (0.0 sigma).
    i.e. Pantheon+ does not DETECT its own assumed lensing term: against a single constant intrinsic
    floor the data prefer NO lensing scatter and put the nominal A = 1 right at the 95% boundary.
    The 1-sigma-equivalent width on A is 0.51 -- the amplitude is simply not measured.
    (Read as a degeneracy, not a tension: Pantheon+'s own model uses a survey-dependent intrinsic
     floor and a redshift-dependent bias-correction scatter, both of which absorb a rising term.
     Allowing that freedom here would only WIDEN the interval on A, never narrow it.)
  
  what would have to be resolved:  the kernel is calibrated to reproduce galaxy-galaxy lensing on
    exactly these scales (the KiDS radial-acceleration test, 0.601 sigma on the record), so the
    framework's convergence field is CONSTRUCTED to sit close to LCDM's where the data already are.
    A generous allowance for the remaining difference -- all matter clumped with baryons instead of
    85% of it in smooth haloes -- is a change in sigma_kappa of order tens of percent, i.e. |A-1| ~ 0.3.
  [FAIL] E2 the supernova lensing-magnification signal has ANY discriminating power between a baryons-plus-kernel convergence field and LCDM's   (the effect to be resolved is |A-1| ~ 0.3; Pantheon+ measures A to +/-0.51, 1.7x too coarse, and does not detect the lensing term itself (0.0 sigma from A = 0). Underpowered by a factor 3; stop here -- this is an honest power calculation, not a result)

======================================================================================================================
F -- BONUS: the low-redshift Hubble-diagram scatter as a direct bound on the peculiar-velocity field
======================================================================================================================
  This is the angle the repo's supernova work has never used.  hunt_2026/h85_bulk_flow_null.py records
  that the framework's kernel, applied naively to the linear velocity field, boosts it by nu ~ 9 at the
  Local Group acceleration -- while the framework's own relativistic completion carries a theorem
  (delta Y^(1) = 0) that makes linear growth LCDM's.  Supernova magnitudes at z < 0.06 are dominated by
  peculiar velocities, so the Hubble diagram bounds that boost independently of any reconstruction.

  567 supernovae at 0.01 < z < 0.06; Pantheon+ assumes a residual peculiar-velocity
    dispersion of 250 km/s after its 2M++ correction.
    fitted multiplier on that dispersion: A_pec = 1.08, 95% upper limit 1.40
    -> residual sigma_v < 350 km/s at 95%.
    (fitted intrinsic floor at the same time: 0.103 mag)
    a naive kernel boost of the velocity field by nu = 9 would give sigma_v ~ 2250 km/s and a magnitude scatter of 0.81 mag at z = 0.02 --
    6x the supernova bound.
    why the 2M++ correction does not rescue the boosted case (stated explicitly, because it is the
    obvious objection): the correction is v_pred = beta x (reconstructed density), with beta
    calibrated in LCDM.  A kernel-boosted velocity-density relation has a LARGER true beta, so
    applying the LCDM beta removes only ~1/nu of the flow and leaves ~8x the LCDM residual --
    which is the quantity the Hubble-diagram scatter measures.  The bound therefore applies to the
    boosted world too; it is not an artefact of correcting the data with the null hypothesis.
  [PASS] F1 the low-redshift supernova Hubble diagram is CONSISTENT with the framework's own linear-growth theorem (LCDM-like velocities) and independently EXCLUDES the naive kernel-boosted velocity field   (sigma_v < 350 km/s vs 2250 km/s for a naive nu = 9 boost; the framework's OWN prediction (delta Y^(1) = 0 -> LCDM growth) passes, the naive reading does not)
  Both ways, plainly: this is a PASS for the framework's own relativistic completion and a KILL for the
  naive modified-gravity reading of the velocity field.  It is a second, independent route to h85's
  null, using a dataset h85 never touched.  It is NOT evidence for the framework over LCDM -- LCDM
  makes the identical prediction and the test cannot separate them.

======================================================================================================================
G -- VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  [FAIL] G1 supernova data, ON THEIR OWN, have live discriminating power for this framework -- i.e. some supernova measurement by itself can move a verdict on a0(z), on the footing fork, or on the kernel   (SN-only evolution 0.52 chi2 for 2 params (0.29 sigma); the SN-era a0 measurements cannot separate the footings through their stated systematics; SN lensing is not detected at all. On the canonical footing SNe enter as an INPUT to (w0, wa), never as a test.)
  [PASS] G2 supernovae nevertheless SHARPEN a framework prediction that another instrument can then decide -- the constructive half of the same finding   (they pin the alt branch to a0(1)/a0(0) = [1.69, 2.15] and the canonical branch's z = 2.5 zero point to +/-0.045 dex; the two branches separate by 0.66 dex at z = 2.5, which the already-pre-registered object decides for free)

  the three-sentence verdict:
  1. Supernovae are an INPUT to this framework, not a test of it: on their own they show no dark-energy
     evolution at all (Delta chi2 = 0.52 for two extra parameters, 77% by chance, 0.29 sigma), the
     canonical-footing SN-only a0(z) band at z = 2.5 is [0.36, 3.59] -- consistent with constant and with
     almost anything else -- and the record's much tighter band is BAO+CMB geometry that supernovae only
     help to close, so the framework's distinctive prediction is hostage to a preference the supernovae
     do not corroborate on their own.
  2. The one place supernovae bite hard is the REGISTERED prediction: the deep-MOND zero point goes as
     a0^{1/4} and a0 tracks rho_DE, so 'flat 0.00 dex at z ~ 2.5' holds only in the w = -1 limit -- under
     every evolving-dark-energy fork on the record the framework's own value is -0.085 to -0.131 dex with a
     0.045 dex within-fork error (66-101% of the registered 0.13 dex budget, and the deepest fork plus
     one sigma reaches 0.176 dex, past it), it appears bare in 8 of 10 places on the record, and it should
     be registered by append-only amendment as a BAND, -0.148 to 0.00 dex, not as a point.
  3. Nothing here favours this framework over LCDM and nothing here is a win: the supernova lensing angle
     is dead on arrival (Pantheon+ does not detect its own lensing term, 0.0 sigma, and is 1.7x too
     coarse for the effect), the supernova-era a0 archive is split 3-3 on the internal footing fork with
     the split falling along MEASUREMENT CURRENCY rather than redshift -- the signature of a z-dependent
     systematic in the slope estimators, not of a0 evolution -- and the one clean new result, the low-z
     Hubble diagram bounding any residual peculiar-velocity boost to under 1.4x, 6x below a naive
     kernel-boosted field, confirms the framework's own linear-growth theorem while making exactly the
     same prediction as LCDM.

======================================================================================================================
SUMMARY: 8 FAIL(s)
  FAIL: B2 the registered 'flat 0.00 dex' central value is UNCHANGED once evolving dark energy is propagated through the exact law with the full (w0, wa) covariance
  FAIL: B4 the registered statistic can be applied as frozen without any risk of scoring the framework's OWN evolving-dark-energy prediction as a falsification of the framework
  FAIL: B5 every place the registered pair (0.00 vs +0.33) appears on the record carries the evolving-dark-energy value alongside it
  FAIL: C3a supernovae ALONE can distinguish the framework's a0(z) from a constant, on the CANONICAL (rho_Lambda) footing
  FAIL: D2 the supernova-era a0 measurements already in the repo (MUSE-DARK III, MSA-3D) can separate the rho_Lambda footing from the rho_total footing
  FAIL: D3 the supernova-era a0 archive leans consistently toward ONE footing, so the fork can at least be given a direction
  FAIL: E2 the supernova lensing-magnification signal has ANY discriminating power between a baryons-plus-kernel convergence field and LCDM's
  FAIL: G1 supernova data, ON THEIR OWN, have live discriminating power for this framework -- i.e. some supernova measurement by itself can move a verdict on a0(z), on the footing fork, or on the kernel
======================================================================================================================
